<v Woman>I mean, it seems like everybody talks about monogamy as something that you do for somebody else, that I will be faithful to you because I love you. But I actually came to feel like non-monogamy was something that you could do for somebody else. You could let them be who they were and still love them and still be committed to them.
<v Woman>我的意思是，似乎每个人都谈论一夫一妻制，认为那是你为别人做的事，因为我爱你，所以我会对你忠诚。但我实际上开始觉得，非一夫一妻制也是你可以为别人做的事。你可以让他们成为他们自己，依然爱他们，依然承诺他们。

00:42:45.170 --> 00:43:25.880
<v Dan Savage>The other assumption that is often made about non-monogamy is that it's a phase. A couple may open their relationship for a time, but they will, once they get serious, return to monogamy. Dr. Rubin's studies have shown that this isn't true either. The couples she followed, all straight and now all in their late 50s and 60s, with houses, mortgages, and kids, as their marriages went on, these couples pretty much stuck to what they'd been doing all along, whether it was monogamy or non-monogamy. So it isn't an experiment or a phase. It's a choice. And like Dave says, it works for the couples that it works for.
<v Dan Savage>关于非一夫一妻制的另一个假设是它是一个阶段。伴侣可能会在一段时间内开放关系，但一旦他们认真起来，就会回归一夫一妻制。鲁宾博士的研究表明这也不是真的。她追踪的伴侣，全是异性恋，现在都在50多岁和60多岁，有房、有贷款、有孩子，随着婚姻的继续，这些伴侣基本上坚持了他们一直在做的事情，无论是一夫一妻制还是非一夫一妻制。所以这不是实验或阶段。这是一种选择。正如戴夫所说，它对那些适用的伴侣来说是有效的。

