00:47:37.460 --> 00:48:20.060
<v Ian Brown>When did all that happen? I can't remember anything. The dates of our courtship, the countless touching things she claims I said, they're at best a blur to me, a fog of declarations. One day, I was living my comfortable life. And the next, she was living with me. On a bad day, it makes me nervous to remember all this. On a bad day, when I think about the slow gelling of our togetherness, its gathering weight and formality, the muscle around my heart closes in and my breath come short and I start to panic. One day, she and I were dating. And then we were living together. And then we bought a house. And then we'd married. And then she wanted to have a baby. An escalator of commitments onto which I had apparently stepped.
<v Ian Brown>这一切是什么时候发生的？我不记得任何事。我们求爱的日子，她声称我说过的无数感人的话，对我来说充其量是一片模糊，是一团宣言的迷雾。有一天，我过着舒适的生活。第二天，她就和我住在一起。在糟糕的日子里，回想起这一切让我紧张。在糟糕的日子里，当我想到我们在一起的缓慢凝固、它聚集的重量和形式感时，我心脏周围的肌肉紧缩，呼吸急促，开始恐慌。有一天，她和我在约会。然后我们住在一起。然后我们买了房子。然后我们结婚了。然后她想要个孩子。这是一场我显然踏上的承诺的自动扶梯。

00:48:20.060 --> 00:49:06.400
<v Ian Brown>My friends were astonished. They said I wasn't the type to marry. A few of them had made bets against it. I told them I wanted "to find out what lay on the other side of boredom." I stole that line from an old girlfriend. I thought I was an adventurer. And yet, here I stand, in a well-trimmed front yard, surrounded by neighbors and swing sets and sport utility vehicles, the movable monuments of domesticity, listening to a couple copulate. And across the yards, I see another man looking warily back at me, doing exactly the same thing.
<v Ian Brown>我的朋友们惊呆了。他们说我不是那种会结婚的人。他们中有几个人赌过我不会结。我告诉他们我想“去看看乏味的另一边是什么”。我从一个老女友那里偷了那句话。我以为我是个冒险家。然而，我站在这里，在修剪得很好的前院里，被邻居、秋千架和运动型多功能车所包围，这些家庭生活的移动纪念碑，听着一对夫妇交媾。而在院子对面，我看到另一个男人警惕地看着我，做着完全一样的事情。

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:58.530
