<v Ian Brown>但我也觉得内心某一部分——不是失望，不是道德愤怒，没有那么大声、尖锐和自以为是，而是——悲伤。就是这个词——悲伤。而且我不确定我知道为什么。我甚至不确定我知道为什么我在努力维持一夫一妻制。为什么我选择了这条路线而不是另一条？我不假装它与道德标准有任何关系。事实上，我怀疑这归结于我妻子曾经对我说的某句话。

00:53:17.940 --> 00:54:08.880
<v Ian Brown>We were having one of our conversational minuets in the dark, one of our gentle but ever so delicate chats about faithfulness, when my wife said that the only thing she missed as a monogamous woman-- at least I assume she was speaking as a monogamous woman-- was newness, new bodies, new hands, new sex. I said I knew what she meant. And I said, "But isn't that kind of sad? I mean, if you go through your whole life, 20, 30, 40, 60 years of marriage without ever straying, you do that, you never get to know what it's like to be unfaithful. You never get to know what it feels like to be emotionally illegal. And that's an important feeling, one of the great human themes, after all, a whole constellation of humanity you'll never know."
<v Ian Brown>我们在黑暗中进行着我们的谈话小步舞，我们关于忠诚的温柔但非常微妙的聊天，当时我的妻子说，作为一个一夫一妻制的女人她错过的唯一东西——至少我假设她是以一个一夫一妻制的女人的身份发言的——是新奇感，新的身体，新的手，新的性。我说我明白她的意思。我说，“但那不是有点悲伤吗？我的意思是，如果你的一生，20、30、40、60年的婚姻都没有出轨，你做到了，你永远无法知道不忠是什么感觉。你永远无法知道在情感上违法是什么感觉。毕竟，那是一种重要的感觉，是伟大的人类主题之一，那是一整个你永远不会知道的人类星群。”

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:43.590
<v Ian Brown>My wife was quiet for a long time. And I could hear the fridge downstairs. And in the street light coming through the curtains, I could just see her outline. And I thought to myself, I've spent a long time in this bed. "Yes," my wife said then, "That's true. But if you do sleep around, you'll never know what it's like to be faithful to one person your whole life, which might also be an 'important constellation of humanity.'" There was just a touch of sarcasm in her voice.
<v Ian Brown>我的妻子沉默了很久。我能听到楼下冰箱的声音。从窗帘中射进来的街灯光中，我只能看到她的轮廓。我对自己想，我在床上待了很长时间。“是的，”我妻子那时说，“那是真的。但如果你真的到处乱搞，你就永远不会知道对一个人忠诚一生是什么感觉，那可能也是一种‘重要的人类星群’。”她的语气里带着一丝讽刺。

00:54:43.590 --> 00:55:16.290
<v Ian Brown>And then it was my turn to lie in the dark for a long time. I'd never considered monogamy as an adventure. I thought it was, well, domestic travel, where no international borders are crossed. But monogamy is an adventure, and in some ways, a more mysterious one than open marriage. Because trying to be faithful to one person is a trip that takes time. And you never really know if you're getting close or if you've reached the destination. You never really know when you've arrived.
<v Ian Brown>然后轮到我躺在黑暗中很久了。我从未把一夫一妻制当作一种冒险。我以为它是，嗯，国内旅行，不会跨越任何国际边界。但一夫一妻制是一种冒险，在某些方面，比开放式婚姻更神秘。因为试图对一个人忠诚是一段需要时间的旅程。你永远不知道你是否接近了，或者你是否到达了目的地。你永远不知道你何时到达了。

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:58.930
<v Ian Brown>For my generation-- the first one that assumed free love for both sexes was a birthright-- for us, monogamy is the last sexual mystery on Earth, the great unknown. We've tried everything else. It's certainly a different kind of trip than open marriage, which has lots of stops along the way. But most of them are the same. Maybe that's the problem with open marriage-- not many surprises. It's titillating, but not very mysterious. Maybe that's why hearing about open marriages always makes me a little sad, why there's always a whiff of defeat about them.
<v Ian Brown>对于我们这一代人——第一代把男女自由恋爱当作与生俱来的权利的一代——对我们来说，一夫一妻制是地球上最后一个性之谜，伟大的未知。我们尝试了一切其他的东西。它当然与开放式婚姻那种沿途有很多停靠点的旅程不同。但其中大多数都是一样的。也许这就是开放式婚姻的问题——惊喜不多。它令人兴奋，但不怎么神秘。也许这就是为什么听说开放式婚姻总是让我有点悲伤，为什么它们总有一种失败的气息。

00:55:58.930 --> 00:56:23.440
<v Ian Brown>I lay there in the dark beside my wife, thinking about all this, as I say, for a long time. I thought about that dream of hers, to be faithful her whole life. I thought, it's a sweet dream, almost like an ideal. And I knew I didn't want to be the one to prove to her that it couldn't come true. It scared me, too, all that responsibility, because it's not such a wise thing to promise.
<v Ian Brown>我躺在妻子身边的黑暗中，思考这一切，如我所说，很长一段时间。我想到了她那个梦想，一生对她忠诚。我想，这是一个甜蜜的梦想，几乎像一种理想。我知道我不想成为那个证明给她看它无法实现的人。它也吓到了我，所有那些责任，因为它不是一个聪明的承诺。

00:56:23.440 --> 00:57:10.760
<v Ian Brown>Anyway, I'm trying to be monogamous, maybe not even for myself. Maybe I'm just doing it for her. Is that so unusual? Maybe if I do it for her long enough, I'll start to do it for me, as well. It's not that I don't want to go to Istanbul anymore. I really want to. In fact, I think about Istanbul all the time, if only to remind myself that it's still there. It's just that there's this other place I want to get to first, a little town that, for the longest time, I never even knew was there.
<v Ian Brown>总之，我正在努力维持一夫一妻制，也许甚至不是为了我自己。也许我只是为了她才这么做。这很反常吗？也许如果我为了她坚持得足够久，我也会开始为自己这么做。不是我不想去伊斯坦布尔了。我真的很想。事实上，我一直都在想伊斯坦布尔，哪怕只是提醒自己它还在那里。只是有另一个地方我想先去，一个小镇，很长一段时间，我甚至不知道它的存在。

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:39.140
<v Ira Glass>Ian Brown is the host of Talking Books on the Sunday edition of CBC's This Morning . Part of his comments are from his book Man Medium Rare.
